    },
    {
      "id": "c5-l2",
      "title": "Counting and Adding",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Loops are perfect for adding things up: start with sum = 0 before the loop, and add one value each round with sum += value.",
        "x += 3 is short for x = x + 3. The same works for -=, *= and /=.",
        "Count the rounds too, and you can work out an average at the end. Remember: to keep the decimals, divide as a double."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int n = 1, sum = 0;",
          "    while (n <= 100) {",
          "        sum += n;",
          "        n++;",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"sum %d\\n\", sum);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "sum 5050"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c5-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is x after x = 4; x += 3;?",
          "options": [
            "7",
            "3",
            "4",
            "12"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "x += 3 adds 3 to x."
        },
        {
          "id": "c5-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why must sum = 0 come before the loop?",
          "options": [
            "Otherwise it would start over every round, or start with garbage",
            "C requires it",
            "To count the rounds",
            "It doesn't matter"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Local variables in C start with whatever was in memory before. Always give a sum its start value."
        },
