    {
      "id": "c5-l3",
      "title": "Reading Input",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "scanf reads what the user types: scanf(\"%d\", &n) reads a whole number into n. %lf reads a double, %c a character.",
        "The & before n is important: it gives scanf the address of n, so it can store the value there. Forget it, and the program crashes. Chapter 5 explains why.",
        "do { … } while (condition); checks at the end, so the body always runs at least once. Perfect for \"ask until the answer makes sense\"."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int n, sum = 0;",
          "    do {",
          "        scanf(\"%d\", &n);",
          "        sum += n;",
          "    } while (n != 0);",
          "    printf(\"total %d\\n\", sum);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "total 12"
        ],
        "inputs": [
          "5",
          "7",
          "0"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c5-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which line reads a whole number into age?",
          "options": [
            "scanf(\"%d\", &age);",
            "scanf(\"%d\", age);",
            "printf(\"%d\", &age);",
            "scanf(age);"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "scanf needs the address of the variable: &age."
        },
        {
          "id": "c5-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What's special about do … while?",
          "options": [
            "The body always runs at least once",
            "It never runs",
            "It runs forever",
            "It can't use scanf"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The condition is checked at the end of each round."
        },
        {
          "id": "c5-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Read a double into price.",
          "code": [
            "    double price;",
            "    scanf(\"___\", &price);"
          ],
          "options": [
            "%lf",
            "%d",
            "%c",
            "%s"
          ],
          "answer": "%lf",
          "explanation": "scanf uses %lf for doubles (printf is fine with %f)."
        },
        {
          "id": "c5-l3-e4",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program crashes when it reads. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int n;",
            "    scanf(\"%d\", n);",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", n * 2);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "scanf needs &n. Without the &, it uses the value of n as an address, and Unix stops the program."
        },
